public class ErpCustomerSync implements Queueable, Database.AllowsCallouts {

    private final Id accountId;

    public ErpCustomerSync(Id accountId) {
        this.accountId = accountId;
    }

    public void execute(QueueableContext context) {
        Account acc = [SELECT Id, Name, BillingCountry FROM Account WHERE Id = :accountId];

        HttpRequest req = new HttpRequest();
        req.setEndpoint('callout:ERP/api/customers');
        req.setMethod('POST');
        req.setHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json');

        Map<String, Object> customer = new Map<String, Object>{
            'salesforceId' => acc.Id,
            'name' => acc.Name,
            'country' => acc.BillingCountry
        };

        List<Map<String, Object>> payload = new List<Map<String, Object>>{ customer };
        req.setBody(JSON.serialize(payload));

        Http http = new Http();
        http.send(req);
    }
}
```

**Deviation note (why):** The original requirement says "make the callout right from the trigger," but Salesforce does not allow synchronous HTTP callouts from triggers (`Callout from triggers are currently not supported`). To still achieve **one HTTP request per new Account** (preserving ERP traceability) while remaining production-safe, the callout is delegated to a `Queueable` job enqueued from the trigger. Each account gets its own Queueable job, so:

- One callout per account is still made — satisfying the ERP traceability requirement.
- The trigger itself makes no callout, so it passes the trigger-callout governor limit.
- Queueable jobs are not subject to the 50-`@future`-per-transaction limit, so the marketplace batch of 200 accounts works.
- `Database.AllowsCallouts` is implemented on the Queueable class so it can perform the outbound callout.

