```apex
public class CaseSurveyJob implements Queueable, Database.AllowsCallouts {

    private List<Id> caseIds;

    // Constructor used by the trigger — receives all newly-closed case IDs
    // collected in a single transaction so we stay within the 50 queueable
    // job-per-transaction governor limit.
    public CaseSurveyJob(List<Id> caseIds) {
        this.caseIds = caseIds;
    }

    public void execute(QueueableContext context) {

        List<Task> tasksToInsert = new List<Task>();

        // Re-query all qualifying cases in the job context to get CaseNumber
        // and confirm ContactId is still present (it may have been deleted
        // between the time the trigger fired and now).
        for (Case c : [SELECT Id, CaseNumber, ContactId
                        FROM   Case
                        WHERE  Id IN :caseIds]) {

            if (c.ContactId == null) {
                continue;
            }

            tasksToInsert.add(new Task(
                WhatId       = c.Id,
                WhoId        = c.ContactId,
                Subject      = 'CSAT survey: ' + c.CaseNumber,
                ActivityDate = Date.today().addDays(2),
                Status       = 'Not Started'
            ));
        }

        if (!tasksToInsert.isEmpty()) {
            insert tasksToInsert;
        }
    }
}
```

### Explanation of key changes and design choices

| Concern | How it is handled |
|---|---|
| **50 queueable job limit** | **Key fix.** The previous version enqueued one `CaseSurveyJob` *per case* inside the trigger loop. When 200 cases are closed in one transaction (bulk list-view close or nightly API cleanup), this hit the 50 queueable-job governor limit and threw `System.LimitException: Too many queueable jobs added to the queue`. Now the trigger collects all qualifying case IDs into a single list and enqueues **one** `CaseSurveyJob` that processes the entire batch. |
| **Bulk-safe trigger** | The trigger loop does no DML — it only builds a `List<Id>`. One `enqueueJob` call happens after the loop, keeping the trigger well within all governor limits regardless of batch size (200, 2 000, or more). |
| **Per-case isolation preserved at the job level** | All Tasks are created in a single `insert` statement inside one Queueable transaction. If you need stronger per-case isolation (e.g. one failure not rolling back others), the `execute` method can iterate the list and insert one Task at a time inside a `try/catch`, or chain subsequent Queueables in batches of ≤ 200. For the current requirement a single bulk insert is appropriate. |
| **Transition detection** | `c.IsClosed && !oldCase.IsClosed` ensures work is only created when a case flips from *open → closed*. Already-closed cases (re-closed via API) and still-open cases are skipped. |
| **Contact guard (double-checked)** | `c.ContactId != null` is checked in the trigger (to avoid unnecessary work) and again inside the Queueable's loop (safety net in case the contact is deleted between the trigger firing and job execution). |
| **`Database.AllowsCallouts`** | Retained so future vendor HTTP callouts can be added to `execute` without requiring a separate Queueable chain. |
| **`CaseNumber` in Subject** | Resolved via fresh SOQL inside the Queueable, guaranteeing the correct value at execution time. |

